<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simla Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1914</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Simla</a> Convention was a treaty negotiated at Simla in 1913–1914 by representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> on the status and borders of Tibet. It proposed dividing Tibet into an autonomous Outer Tibet and an Inner Tibet under closer Chinese control. China's delegate initialled the draft but his government refused to sign, and Britain and Tibet signed it alone, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-line">McMahon Line</a> border agreement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simla-convention.jpg" alt="Simla Convention" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simla_Conference_1913.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
